# PHASE 2 — PROJECT CONTEXT

## Core problem

Indian Railways operates mixed traffic involving Vande Bharat, Rajdhani, other passenger services, and freight trains.

Section controllers must manage:

- Dense train traffic
- Conflicting train movements
- Variable running speeds
- Signal and block restrictions
- Train precedence
- Delays
- Track availability
- Station throat bottlenecks
- Weather-related operational constraints
- Cascading disruptions
- Timetable reorganization

Traditional information views may not present these relationships in a sufficiently predictive or explainable manner.

RailRaksha proposes an AI-assisted, human-in-the-loop decision-support system to help a controller understand developing conflicts, compare feasible movement alternatives and assess their effects.

## Existing academic project

RailRaksha currently includes or has explored:

- Babylon.js 3D digital twin
- Railway track geometry and train movement
- Indian railway rolling-stock GLB models
- Train identity and priority
- Multiple tracks and block sections
- Signal state changes
- Time-to-collision (TTC)
- Train separation monitoring
- Closing speed
- Physics-inspired braking logic
- CLEAR / CAUTION / DANGER classifications
- Weather scenarios
- Artificial conflict injection
- Traffic and congestion scenarios
- AI-inspired optimization controls
- Controller acceptance and modification of simulated plans
- Timetable operations
- Scenario replay
- Synthetic telemetry generation
- Random Forest
- CatBoost
- XGBoost
- Research benchmarking of eleven supervised ML models
- SHAP and LIME explanations
- FastAPI backend
- Third-party RailRadar train information
- Local data caching and quota protection
- Historical train-status snapshots
- Multilingual announcements

The research describes six train instances and a 10 Hz, 12 km simulation, while later browser implementations may use eight services. Preserve the distinction instead of silently reconciling these into fictional operational figures.

The existing ML experiments use synthetic training data. Their predictions must not be represented as certified real-world collision forecasts.

