# PHASE 11 — REAL-TIME DATA AND API INTEGRATION

## Existing external provider

The project already has an integrated RailRadar train-status API.

Reuse its existing backend integration, environment configuration, caching and request counters if present.

Do not delete it or replace it with an LLM.

Real railway running status and generative AI are different services.

RailRadar handles external train information.

The LLM helps explain existing data.

## API request management

The existing project uses conservative local provider-call limits:

- 12 calls per day
- 240 calls per month
- Manual refresh
- Response caching
- Up to three train numbers per fleet refresh

Inspect the actual implementation and preserve or strengthen these safeguards.

These are application safeguards, not a guarantee of the provider's real allowance.

Add:

- Visible quota remaining
- Last successful provider request
- Cache age
- Provider unavailable state
- Source classification
- Stale-data warnings
- No unnecessary background polling
- Train-specific duplicate-request suppression
- Request counting before outbound calls

Never introduce a dashboard render loop that calls the train provider repeatedly.

## Secure credentials

Keep API keys only in server-side environment variables.

Never include keys in HTML, browser JavaScript, screenshots, Git history or downloadable frontend bundles.

Use a `.env.example` file with placeholder names.

The website should support cached/demo mode when credentials are absent.

## Accurate data provenance

Every displayed metric must belong to one of these categories:

- THIRD_PARTY_REPORTED
- SIMULATED
- ML_PREDICTED_SYNTHETIC
- CONTROLLER_DECISION
- DERIVED_ESTIMATE
- CACHED

Show the source, timestamp and validity level wherever it affects interpretation.

Do not label third-party reported data as verified track telemetry or official signalling information.

